            {
              "id": "CODE-QV-ASYMMETRIC",
              "name": "CodeQvAsymmetric",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Quantum-vulnerable RSA: authentication and identity (certificates, tokens, device attestation) (5 file(s))"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "post_quantum",
                  "pq_long_term_risk"
                ],
                "security-severity": "5.0"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "8 reference(s) to RSA across 5 file(s), e.g. obp-api/src/main/scala/code/api/util/BerlinGroupSigning.scala:95. Role, judged from paths, function names and nearby code: authentication and identity (certificates, tokens, device attestation). Short-lived authentication stays safe until a CRQC exists; long-lived identities (device attestation, CA and SSH keys) need a plan earlier. Keep credentials short-lived and plan ML-DSA certificates, tokens and keys as the ecosystem supports them. This is a forward-looking risk that depends on the arrival of a cryptographically relevant quantum computer (CRQC). No such computer is publicly known to exist today; the timeline is uncertain. It is not a present-day exploitable vulnerability. Heuristic pattern match on source text without data-flow analysis. The usage context (security-relevant or not) must be confirmed manually; treat as preliminary."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "8 reference(s) to RSA across 5 file(s), e.g. obp-api/src/main/scala/code/api/util/BerlinGroupSigning.scala:95. Role, judged from paths, function names and nearby code: authentication and identity (certificates, tokens, device attestation). Short-lived authentication stays safe until a CRQC exists; long-lived identities (device attestation, CA and SSH keys) need a plan earlier. Keep credentials short-lived and plan ML-DSA certificates, tokens and keys as the ecosystem supports them. This is a forward-looking risk that depends on the arrival of a cryptographically relevant quantum computer (CRQC). No such computer is publicly known to exist today; the timeline is uncertain. It is not a present-day exploitable vulnerability. Heuristic pattern match on source text without data-flow analysis. The usage context (security-relevant or not) must be confirmed manually; treat as preliminary."
